            "text": "{\n  \"relation\": \"MATCH\",\n  \"consensus_summary\": \"The consensus identifies the white horse as a symbol of victory, conquest, and judgment. In Revelation 19, it is universally seen as Christ's mount; in Revelation 6, it represents conquest (variously interpreted as Christ, the Antichrist, or generic militarism); and in Zechariah, colored horses act as divinely commissioned executing agents or spirits.\",\n  \"book_summary\": \"The book defines the white horse as the mount of an executing spirit and a conqueror who wins nations by the bow.\",\n  \"consensus_core_identification\": \"A symbol of conquest, victorious power, and divine agency.\",\n  \"book_core_identification\": \"A mount for an executing spirit and a conqueror with a bow.\",\n  \"core_relation\": \"EQUIVALENT\",\n  \"extension_relation\": \"BOOK_SUBSET\",\n  \"boundary_disagreement\": \"The book focuses strictly on the imagery of Revelation 6:2 and its background in Zechariah 6 (executing spirits and a conqueror with a bow), omitting the broadly recognized Revelation 19 appearance (Christ conquering with a sword).\",\n  \"material_contradiction\": \"None. The book's identifications directly reflect established consensus views of the Revelation 6 and Zechariah passages.\",\n  \"rationale\": \"The book synthesizes the Zechariah 6 imagery of colored horses as heavenly spirits/agents with the Revelation 6:2 imagery of the rider with a bow. This exactly matches consensus interpretations of those specific passages, though it serves as a narrower subset by excluding Revelation 19.\",\n  \"citation_support\": \"STRONG\",\n  \"strongest_objection\": \"By omitting Revelation 19, the book ignores the most universally agreed-upon and prominent appearance of a white horse in the Bible, potentially providing an incomplete definition of the symbol.\",\n  \"confidence\": \"high\"\n}",
